<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Kamal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Al-Bukamal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Kamal (al-Bukamal) is a town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, in Deir ez-Zor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> at the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, opposite al-Qa'im. The ruins of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> at Tell Hariri lie a few kilometres to the north-west. Its border crossing has made it strategically important in modern conflicts, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-civil-war">Syrian civil war</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-kamal.jpg" alt="Abu Kamal" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agagasoft · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Salihiyah-Al-Bukamal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Abu Kamal, also called Al-Bukamal, is the administrative centre of the Abu Kamal District and Subdistrict. Just to the south-east is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaim">Al-Qa'im</a> border crossing to the town of Husaybah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s Al Anbar Governorate. Under Ottoman rule the place was called kışla, a Turkish word for military barracks, still pronounced locally as qashla. The name Al-Bukamal means the family of Kamal, the tribe that lives there, while Abu Kamal means the father of Kamal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 17th century Abu Kamal was the seat of a sanjak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the Rakka Eyalet, and until the end of Ottoman rule in 1918 it was a kaza centre within the Zor Sanjak. In 1896 the old mud village was moved a mile upstream because flooding threatened it, and the new village was built of stone with wide streets. It was the centre of the Arab Uqaydat tribe and largely Sunni Muslim, with a small Christian community. Early in the 20th century it had eighty houses, a ferry and about 500 people. It became part of the French Mandate in 1921, when the French made it the seat of a large garrison, and part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1946.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq-war">Iraq War</a>, on 27 October 2008, four US helicopters raided the village of Sukkariyeh in Abu Kamal district, the first known US raid inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. At least seven people were killed, four of them children, and the Syrian Foreign Minister Walid Muallem called killing civilians a terrorist aggression under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-civil-war">Syrian civil war</a> the Free Syrian Army took the town in late July 2012, and the nearby Hamdan military airport on 16 November 2012. Islamic State forces captured it between late June and early July 2014, and it saw little fighting until 2017.</p><p>The Syrian Army began an operation against the town on 7 November 2017, backed by allied forces and Russian air strikes. Islamic State fighters retreated on 9 November, retook the town on 11 November, and the army and its allies regained complete control by 19 November. From late 2017 Iranian-allied militias occupied the town and countryside. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaim">Al-Qa'im</a> crossing reopened on 30 September 2019 after eight years of closure. On 27 October 2023 the US carried out air strikes on bases near the town, which it said were used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-revolutionary-guard-corps">Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps</a>, and on 10 December 2024 Syrian opposition forces announced that they had captured it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Kamal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mari stood at Tell Hariri, near the modern town of <b>Abu Kamal</b> in eastern Syria, on the right bank of the middle Euphrates close to the present border with Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
